    "title": "Nebraska Senator Ricketts criticizes Argentina's soybean deal with China - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Kayla Samek Maren Ewertz ,\n1 hours ago\nNEBRASKA ( KCAU ) — China typically buys around 40% of all soybeans grown in the United States, but the ongoing issue with tariffs and the Trump administration’s wanting countries to pay more for American goods has made it cheaper for countries to go elsewhere.\nLast week, the U.S. government announced that it would be sending $20 billion to bail out Argentina’s government. As a result, the country removed export taxes on soybeans, and sold a quarter of its crop to China.\nStory continues below\nNebraska Senator Pete Ricketts said Argentina did not act in good faith after receiving the bailout.\n“I do think it was bad form for them to turn around right after we decided to help them out to sell soybeans to China, and especially taking the taxes off of it to reduce the price further,” Senator Ricketts said. “I think that does require that we have some conversations with our friends about what our expectations are when we’re helping them out.”\nThe administration is promising economic aid to farmers impacted by the trade war with China, but the government shutdown means the details of the aid cannot be finalized.\nCopyright 2025 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to SiouxlandProud | Sioux City, IA | News, Weather, and Sports.",
